The <ansi fg="command">questtoken</ansi> command manages quest
progress for your character.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

<ansi fg="command">questtoken list</ansi>
  Show your current quest progress. Each quest displays
  all steps, with your current step in [brackets].

<ansi fg="command">questtoken all</ansi>
  List every quest in the game and all their step tokens.
  Useful for finding the exact token name to grant.

<ansi fg="command">questtoken [questid-step]</ansi>
  Grant yourself a quest token, advancing (or starting)
  a quest. Triggers rewards if the step is the final one.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Token format:</ansi> <ansi fg="cyan">questid-stepname</ansi>
  Examples: <ansi fg="command">questtoken 8-start</ansi>
            <ansi fg="command">questtoken 8-end</ansi>
            <ansi fg="command">questtoken 12-ritual</ansi>

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Common uses:</ansi>
  - Unstick a player whose quest bricked (grant the
    next step they should be on)
  - Skip to the end of a quest for testing
  - Set up prerequisites before testing a later quest
    (e.g. <ansi fg="command">questtoken 11-end</ansi> before testing Q12)

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Notes:</ansi>
  - Granting an end step triggers quest rewards (gold,
    items, spells) just like normal completion.
  - Use <ansi fg="command">questtoken list</ansi> first to see current
    state before making changes.
  - There is no undo. To "reset" a quest, the character
    data must be edited directly.
